id: recipe.async-job-processing
kind: recipe
title: Async job processing
aliases: async job processing, background jobs, job queue
question: how would you design a service receiving 100k events per minute and processing them asynchronously?
tags: architecture, queue, batch
properties:
  - admitted load is decoupled from processing rate
  - bounded queue depth (no unbounded memory growth)
  - horizontal scaling of workers
  - idempotent processing under redelivery
  - graceful shutdown with no lost work
invariant: Accept fast, persist durably, process with bounded concurrency, and make every job idempotent so redelivery is free.
answer_30s: A thin ingest API that validates and enqueues into a durable bounded queue, plus worker replicas that pull jobs with bounded concurrency, retry idempotently, and shut down by draining.
flow: |
  Ingress / API (validate + enqueue, fast)
     |
  durable queue (bounded depth, backpressure at the edge)
     |
  worker deployment (N consumers, bounded concurrency)
     |
  database (idempotent writes keyed by job id)
     |
  metrics: queue depth, in-flight, per-item latency
compose:
  - design.producer-consumer => ingest and processing scale and fail independently
  - design.bounded-queue => depth is the saturation signal, and overflow has a policy
  - design.backpressure => when workers fall behind, the producer slows or the edge sheds
  - design.worker-pool => concurrency is a chosen number, not a function of traffic
  - design.idempotency => redelivery and retries cannot duplicate effects
  - design.retry-backoff => transient failures retry with jitter and a cap
  - design.state-machine => each job has an explicit state, so stuck jobs are visible
  - design.graceful-shutdown => deploys do not lose in-flight jobs
  - k8s.deployment => worker replicas roll and scale independently of the API
  - k8s.hpa => scale on queue depth or in-flight work, not CPU alone
  - k8s.resources.requests => scheduler places workers predictably
  - rust.tokio-mpsc => bounded channel is the in-process half of the same contract
  - rust.tokio-semaphore => in-flight bound that protects the shared database
  - rust.graceful-shutdown => stop pulling, finish the item, acknowledge, exit
failure_modes:
  - unbounded queue, so a worker outage becomes an OOM instead of a visible backlog
  - non-idempotent jobs, so a redelivery double-charges or double-sends
  - workers scaled on CPU while the queue grows (queue depth is the real signal)
  - no dead-letter path, so one poison job blocks the pipeline
follow_ups:
  - what do you do when the queue is growing and workers are at max replicas?
related:
  - design.bounded-queue
  - design.worker-pool
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/
